nio: block the boot thread instead of a may-be-noop yield

CI 640 printed "YIELD to PID1 thread" but nothing followed.  The reason
is in syscall_subr.c: thread_yield_internal() first calls
SCHED(thread_should_yield)() and returns immediately when the scheduler
does not consider another thread worth switching to.  With the init
thread's PATH flags stuck at 0x150, that early return is exactly what
happened: no context switch, no URET, no TWR.

Replace it with an unconditional thread_block(THREAD_CONTINUE_NULL) at
the same point (the end of bsd_init, after exec and after all kernel
setup).  This forces thread_select() to choose somebody.  If the init
thread is really on a run queue, it now runs task_wait_to_return ->
thread_bootstrap_return -> EL0 with launchd already installed in its
task.  If it is not runnable, we will see the new BLOCK marker and then
silence, which itself is the answer.

No exec or SMP behaviour changes.


diff --git a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
index 642bd10..759c866 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
@@ -1292,8 +1292,8 @@ bsd_init(void)
 	 * exec image is already installed in the init task, so
 	 * task_wait_to_return -> thread_bootstrap_return should enter
 	 * launchd in EL0.  If this thread never comes back, that is the goal. */
-	printf("\nnio: YIELD to PID1 thread\n");
-	thread_yield_internal(1);
+	printf("\nnio: BLOCK boot thread for PID1\n");
+	thread_block(THREAD_CONTINUE_NULL);
 }
 
 #if defined(XNU_OWNER_ROOT_AUDIT) && XNU_OWNER_ROOT_AUDIT
